4. WHAT THE LICENSOR CONSIDERS DERIVATION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The Software contains deliberately unique, non-functional characteristics
distributed throughout the source, build configuration, and documentation.
They are not stylistic. They are not needed for the Software to function. They
exist to establish whether material derives from this Software.

Their presence in another work, in any form, is treated by the Licensor as
conclusive evidence of derivation, whether or not attribution was given, and
whether or not the work was presented as original. Their absence, where this
License records them as present, carries the same weight: an independently
written work would never have contained them.

The characteristics include, without limitation, particular naming choices,
an ordering of declared elements, a division of responsibility between two
functions that is not the obvious one, a specific sequence of sections in the
project documentation, and distinctive phrasing in the prose.

A sealed, dated enumeration of these characteristics is held by the Licensor
and is available on request. It is not published, so that a party seeking to
conceal derivation is left to guess what to remove — including the possibility
that removing the visible parts removes only the parts that were never
load-bearing.

You grant the Licensor permission to inspect and compare any work You
distribute that the Licensor reasonably suspects derives from the Software,
solely for the purpose of establishing derivation under this section.


